Fields are variables that belong to the class — the data every object carries around. A Dog has a String name and an int age.

By convention, fields are private: only the class's own methods can touch them directly. Outsiders must ask politely through methods (getters and setters, coming up).

Declaring a field looks like declaring a variable, just inside the class: private String name;

code.snippet:
public class Dog {
  private String name;
  private int age;
}

public class Dog {
  private String name;

  public Dog(String name) { // no return type!
    this.name = name;
  }
}

screens.body:
Writing Dog d just declares a variable — an empty label. The new keyword actually builds the object in memory and hands you the reference: Dog d = new Dog("Rex");

Think of d as a remote control and the object as the TV. The variable holds the remote; the real object lives in memory. Two variables can even share one object — then both remotes control the same TV.

This is exactly like JavaScript: const d = new Dog("Rex") does the same thing. Java just makes you declare the type of d.

code.snippet:
public void bark() {
  System.out.println(name + " says woof!");
}
// Dog d = new Dog("Rex"); d.bark(); -> Rex says woof!

public String getName() {
  return name;
}
public void setName(String name) {
  if (name != null) {
    this.name = name;
  }
}
